  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nShowers associated with some strong low/mid level frontogenesis \nforcing will continue to exit northwest Ohio the remainder \nafternoon. The primary focus for additional rainfall will remain \nback across northwest Indiana and southwest Lower Michigan where \nsecondary surface trough associated with strong positively tilted \nupper trough is augmenting lake effect rain showers. The focus for \nthese lake effect/enhanced rain showers will shift to northwest \nIndiana this evening as low level winds veer northerly behind the \nsecondary sfc trough. A Wind Advisory remains in effect through this \nevening for LaPorte and Berrien Counties, although the window for 35 \nmph plus gusts may be confined to a 20 to 23Z timeframe. \nBoundary layer mixing may be tempered a bit by the very moist \nprofiles in place, and thus confidence is beginning to diminish\nsomewhat in realizing advisory level winds, particularly after \n00Z as a lake-induced thermal trough is likely to sharpen and \nweaken the low level height gradient. At this time will continue\nthe Wind Advisory and will defer to next shift to monitor for \npossible cancellation depending on how trends evolve. Lake \neffect rain showers may persist into the early overnight hours \nacross northwest Indiana as thermal troughing response over \nsouthern Lake Michigan matures this evening.\n\nThe positively tilted nature of the upper trough should allow for \nsynoptic scale mid level subsidence to build in quickly later \nthis evening and overnight resulting in expected diminishing \ncloud trend outside of lingering lake effect. Low levels should\nremain well mixed into the early evening hours with some better\npotential of decoupling overnight. This introduces the risk of \nsome patchy frost development and temperatures dropping into \nthe low-mid 30s tonight. The best chance of frost formation \nstill appears to be across much of southern Lower Michigan into \nportions of north central Indiana situated between more delayed \ncloud cover decrease in the west with lake effect, and slightly \nbetter mixing persisting east of I-69 into the overnight. \n\nQuiet weather is expected on Sunday as the southern Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley will be positioned downstream of broad longwave ridge \naxis across central CONUS allowing for extended synoptic scale \nsubsidence. Sfc anticyclone will overspread the region late \nSaturday afternoon into Saturday evening with some lighter \nsouthwest return flow setting up across the western Great Lakes \nby Sunday morning. Frost/freeze potential will need to be \nconsidered again for this period. Some minor modification of low\nlevel thermal trough is expected Saturday but mainly clear \nskies and light winds should allow for good radiational cooling \nSaturday night and a potential of temperatures dropping to the \nfreezing mark. Best chances based on timing of the high pressure\nstill appear to be east of I-69 where a Freeze Watch has been \nissued. Some combination of Frost Advisories and/or Freeze \nWarnings will likely be needed Saturday night.\n\nUpper flow pattern will become more stagnant and blocked in \nnature for much of next week with little overall progression in \nmean positioning of central CONUS ridge. Gradual day to day \nmoderation and max temperatures in upper 60s to lower 70s and \ndry conditions will prevail for much of next week. Cannot \ncompletely rule out some patchy frost concerns Sunday and Monday\nnights, but this is a low confidence item at this time.",
